        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "380823",
          "text": "<p>We consider log to the base of $2.$ Thus for $\\log \\mathrm{N}$ to be a natural number, $\\mathrm{N}$ must be a power of $2.$ Let $\\mathrm{N}$ be an arbitrary number with $\\mathrm{N}&gt;1$. Which one of the following statements is true? (Assume root is at height zero)</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>A binary tree of height $\\log \\mathrm{N}$ can have exactly $\\mathrm{N}$ nodes.</li>\n    <li>A binary tree of height $\\log \\mathrm{N}$ can have exactly $\\log \\mathrm{N}$ nodes.</li>\n    <li>A binary tree of height $\\log \\mathrm{N}$ can have exactly $\\mathrm{N} \\log \\mathrm{N}$ nodes.</li>\n    <li>A binary tree of height $\\log \\mathrm{N}$ can have exactly $\\mathrm{N}^{2}$ nodes.</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2024_wq27,goclasses,data-structures,binary-tree,two-marks",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "380815",
          "text": "<p>Which of the following is/are true?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>Suppose $\\mathrm{T}$ is a completely filled binary search tree with $7$ nodes, the worst-case number of comparisons for a successful find operation in $\\mathrm{T}$ is $3$</li>\n    <li>Suppose $\\mathrm{T}$ is a completely filled binary search tree with $7$ nodes. the best case number of comparisons for a successful find operation in $\\mathrm{T}$ is $1$</li>\n    <li>Suppose $\\mathrm{T}$ is a binary search tree with $7$ nodes, only one of which is a leaf. suppose all keys are equally likely, the integer closest to the average case number of comparisons for a successful find operation in $\\mathrm{T}$ is $4$</li>\n    <li>Suppose $\\mathrm{T}$ is a completely filled binary search tree with $7$ nodes. suppose all keys are equally likely. The integer closest to the average case number of comparisons for a successful find operation in $\\mathrm{T}$ is $2$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "a;b;c;d",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses2024_wq27,goclasses,data-structures,binary-search-tree,multiple-selects,two-marks",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "380832",
          "text": "<p>Inorder traversing a tree resulted $\\text{E A C K F H D B G};$ the preorder traversal would return.</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>$\\text{F A E K C D B H G}$</li><li>$\\text{F A E K C D H G B}$</li><li>$\\text{E A F K H D C B G}$</li><li>$\\text{F E A K D C H B G}$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2024_wq27,data-structures,tree-traversal,one-mark",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "380826",
          "text": "<p>Suppose you wanted to insert the following elements into a binary search tree of integers: $90,3,16,7,12,38,50$. Which of the following insertion orders will result in a <em>complete binary tree?</em></p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$16,7,3,12,50,38,90$</li>\n    <li>$16,38,3,12,50,90,7$</li>\n    <li>$7,16,3,90,12,50,38$</li>\n    <li>$16,3,12,50,90,7,38$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2024_wq27,goclasses,data-structures,binary-search-tree,two-marks",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "380827",
          "text": "<p>Consider a sorted circular doubly-linked list where the head element points to the smallest element in the list.</p>\n\n<p>Which of the following is/are true?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$O(1)$ is the asymptotic&nbsp; complexity of finding the smallest element in the list</li>\n    <li>$O(1)$ is the asymptotic&nbsp; complexity of finding the largest element in the list</li>\n    <li>$O(n)$ is the asymptotic&nbsp; complexity of determining whether a given element $e$ appears in the list</li>\n    <li>$O(1)$ is the asymptotic complexity of deleting a given element $e$ in the list (not including the cost of finding it)</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "a;b;c;d",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses2024_wq27,goclasses,data-structures,linked-list,time-complexity,multiple-selects,one-mark",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "380825",
          "text": "<p>In a level order traversal of a tree, nodes are traversed level-wise from the root and left to right in a level. For example, the level order traversal of the binary tree shown below is $\\mathrm{X}, \\mathrm{Y}, \\mathrm{Z}, \\mathrm{E}, \\mathrm{C}, \\mathrm{D}, \\mathrm{A}, \\mathrm{B}$.</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align:center\"><img alt=\"\" height=\"193\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" width=\"329\"></p>\n\n<p><br>\nWhich one of the following combinations uniquely defines a binary tree?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>Level-order and pre-order traversals</li>\n    <li>Level-order and in-order traversals</li>\n    <li>Level-order and post-order traversals</li>\n    <li>Pre-order and post-order traversals</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses2024_wq27,goclasses,data-structures,tree-traversal,multiple-selects,two-marks",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "380820",
          "text": "<p>Consider the following equation, where $\\mathrm{N}$ is the number of nodes in a particular binary search tree, and $d\\left(x_{i}\\right)$ is the depth of node $x_{i}$ in the tree. What does this equation compute?</p>\n\n<p>Let All nodes in the tree are equally likely to be searched for.<br>\n$$<br>\n\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{\\mathrm{N}} d\\left(x_{i}\\right)\\right) / \\mathrm{N}<br>\n$$</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>The average depth of a node in a specific BST with $\\mathrm{N}$ nodes</li>\n    <li>The average depth of a node in the average BST with $\\mathrm{N}$ nodes</li>\n    <li>The total depth of all nodes in a specific BST with $\\mathrm{N}$ nodes</li>\n    <li>The average total depth of all nodes in all BSTs with $\\mathrm{N}$ nodes</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2024_wq27,goclasses,data-structures,binary-search-tree,two-marks",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "380806",
          "text": "<p>Which of the following is is/are true ?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>The depths of any two leaves in a max heap differ by at most $1.$</li>\n    <li>Inserting into an AVL tree with n nodes requires $\\Theta(\\log n)$ rotations.</li>\n    <li>The height of any binary search tree with $n$ nodes is $O(\\log n).$</li>\n    <li>We can convert a heap to Binary Search Tree in $\\Theta(n)$.</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "A",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses2024_wq27,goclasses,data-structures,binary-search-tree,avl-tree,binary-heap,multiple-selects,two-marks",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "380816",
          "text": "<p>Given a binary search tree $\\mathrm{T}$, we insert $n$ elements, but when the size of tree become doubled then we balance the tree. for example if we insert $2^{k-1}$ element then when the size become to $2^{k}$ then we balance $\\mathrm{T}.$ what is tightest time complexity insert $n$ items in such tree?</p><ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\"><li>$\\mathrm{O}(n)$</li><li>$\\mathrm{O}\\left(n^{2}\\right)$</li><li>$\\mathrm{O}(n \\log n)$</li><li>$\\mathrm{O}\\left(n^{2} \\log n \\right)$</li></ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2025-cs-mockgate-14,goclasses,data-structures,binary-search-tree,time-complexity,two-marks",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "380811",
          "text": "Number of AVL tree(s) containing elements with keys $2, 4, 6, 8,$ and $10.$",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "6",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses2024_wq27,numerical-answers,goclasses,data-structures,avl-tree,two-marks",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "409217",
          "text": "<p>Suppose that a binary min-heap stores six elements with priorities $10, 20, 30, 40, 50,$ and $60$ in its array $\\text{A}.$ What is the largest of these items that could be stored in $\\text{A}[1]?$ (indexing starts from zero)</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$20$</li>\n    <li>$30$</li>\n    <li>$40$</li>\n    <li>$50$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "C",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses2024_wq27,goclasses,data-structures,binary-heap,two-marks",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "380817",
          "text": "<p>Suppose we have a complete binary tree, $\\mathrm{X}$, that is not a heap, and we wish to heapify it. The obvious thing to do is just insert all nodes of $\\mathrm{X}$ into a new binary heap $\\mathrm{Y}$. This works, but it doubles the space requirement. Suppose we'd prefer to heapify in place. Which of the following procedures will convert $\\mathrm{X}$ into a heap (containing all of the original values)?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>Sink (heapify down) all nodes in level order (first the root, then its left child, then the right child of the root, etc).</li>\n    <li>Swim (heapify up) all nodes in level order.</li>\n    <li>Swim all nodes in reverse level order.</li>\n    <li>Sink all nodes in reverse level order.</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "b;d",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses2024_wq27,goclasses,data-structures,binary-heap,multiple-selects,two-marks",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "380831",
          "text": "<p>We found a new implementation of priority queue where insert takes constant time and $\\textsf{extractMin}$ takes $O(\\log n)$ time. Which of the following is true?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>This can be used to sort in constant time</li>\n    <li>This can be used to sort in linear time</li>\n    <li>This can be used to sort in $O(n \\log n)$ time</li>\n    <li>For sorting, one needs a hash table,&nbsp;not a priority queue</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2024_wq27,goclasses,data-structures,priority-queue,one-mark",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "380828",
          "text": "<p>Consider a binary search tree whose labels are each one capital letter, and assume that a level-order traversal yields the sequence $\\text{\"GENIUS\"}.$ What is the preorder traversal of this tree?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$\\text{GENIUS}$</li>\n    <li>$\\text{GNEUIS}$</li>\n    <li>$\\text{GUINES}$</li>\n    <li>$\\text{GIUSEN}$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2024_wq27,goclasses,data-structures,binary-search-tree,tree-traversal,one-mark",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "380830",
          "text": "<p>Let $\\mathrm{C}(\\mathrm{X})$ be the number of leaves in a binary tree rooted at $\\mathrm{T}$. Assume that $\\mathrm{IsLeaf (T)}$ returns $1$ if $\\mathrm{T}$ is a leaf. Which of the following observations leads to a recursive implementation?</p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n    <li>$\\mathrm{C(T):=C(T . Left )+C( T. Right )}$</li>\n    <li>$\\mathrm{C(T):=C(T . Left )+C(T. Right )+1}$</li>\n    <li>$\\mathrm{C(T):=C(T . Left )+C(T. Right )+ IsLeaf (T)}$</li>\n    <li>$\\mathrm{C(T):=C(T . Left )+C(T . Right )+ IsLeaf (T)+1}$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses2024_wq27,goclasses,data-structures,binary-tree,recurrence-relation,one-mark",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
